# `CLASS-CHANNEL-REACH`: a class must not declare paint that cannot reach the
# member it joins, and the test is against the channel set that member's
# DRAWING reads — not against what the directive tables accept. This case
# pins the two edges of that rule at once:
#
#   REJECT  a class whose channels are ALL channels the member lacks
#           (`style=`-only on a `field` or a `cell`: `style=` left both
#           directives, `STYLE-KEY-SCOPE`, and no dash is drawn from a class
#           on either) and `fill=` with no `stroke=` on an interior-less
#           member (`INTERIOR-LESS-ELEMENT-PAINT`, 0.1, unchanged).
#   ACCEPT  a class that claims a MEANING and declares NO paint, on any
#           member. That was a line error on an `edge` to
#           0.4 (`CLASS-PAINT-REQUIREMENT`'s second half) and is legal.
#           The legend draws its meaning with no swatch, which is what the
#           `field`-joined meaning-only classes in examples/gre.fd,
#           examples/quic.fd and examples/srh.fd have always relied on.
figdown 0.1 bitfield
class fdash "Vendor-specific" style=dotted
class fmean "Present only when the C bit is 1"
bitfield w "Word" numbering=msb0
field "A":16 class=fdash
field "B":16 class=fmean

figdown 0.1 table
class cdash "Vendor-specific" style=dotted
class cmean "Deprecated in the 2019 revision"
table t "Registers"
| Name | Width |
|---|---|
| CTRL | 32 |
| STAT | 32 |
cell (1,1) class=cdash
cell (2,1) class=cmean

figdown 0.1 block
class emean "Physical underlay link"
class efill "Hot path" fill=#fee2e2 style=dashed
class eboth "Overlay tunnel" fill=#fee2e2 stroke=#dc2626
node a "A"
node b "B"
node c "C"
node d "D" class=efill
edge a -> b class=emean
edge a -> c class=efill
edge a -> d class=eboth
